eractions between two PB1 domains. <source>J. Mol. Biol.</source> <volume>336</volume>:<fpage>1195</fpage>&#x02013;1210.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15037079</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib44"><citation citation-type="journal">Wang, F., P. Herzmark, O.D. Weiner, S. Srinivasan, G. Servant, and H.R. Bourne. <year>2002</year>. Lipid products of PI(3)Ks maintain persistent cell polarity and directed motility in neutrophils. <source>Nat. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>4</volume>:<fpage>513</fpage>&#x02013;518.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12080345</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib45"><citation citation-type="journal">Wedlich-Soldner, R., and R. Li. <year>2003</year>. Spontaneous cell polarization: undermining determinism. <source>Nat. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>5</volume>:<fpage>267</fpage>&#x02013;270.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12669070</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib46"><citation citation-type="journal">Wedlich-Soldner, R., M. Bolker, R. Kahmann, and G. Steinberg. <year>2000</year>. A putative endosomal t-SNARE links exo- and endocytosis in the phytopathogenic fungus Ustilago maydis. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>19</volume>:<fpage>1974</fpage>&#x02013;1986.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10790364</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib47"><citation citation-type="journal">Wedlich-Soldner, R., S. Altschuler, L. Wu, and R. Li. <year>2003</year>. Spontaneous cell polarization through actomyosin-based delivery of the Cdc42 GTPase. <source>Science.</source> <volume>299</volume>:<fpage>1231</fpage>&#x02013;1235.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12560471</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib48"><citation citation-type="journal">Weiner, O.D., P.O. Neilsen, G.D. Prestwich, M.W. Kirschner, L.C. Cantley, and H.R. Bourne. <year>2002</year>. A PtdInsP(3)- and Rho GTPase-mediated positive feedback loop regulates neutrophil polarity. <source>Nat. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>4</volume>:<fpage>509</fpage>&#x02013;513.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12080346</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="